      "dom_id": "question-11843",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given that $y=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{{x}^{3}+1},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)={e}^{3x}$ and the denominator $v(x)={x}^{3}+1.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=3{e}^{3x},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3{e}^{3x})({x}^{3}+1)-({e}^{3x})(3{x}^{2})}{({x}^{3}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{3}+1){e}^{3x}-3{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}}{({x}^{3}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(({x}^{3}+1)-{x}^{2}){e}^{3x}}{({x}^{3}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+1){e}^{3x}}{{({x}^{3}+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
