      "dom_id": "question-11942",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given that $y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)={x}^{3}$ and the denominator $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1).$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3{x}^{2})(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1))-({x}^{3})(\\frac{2}{2x+1})}{{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{2x+1}}{{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1))}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
